1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,918
Ava: What if your accelerator could run PyTorch models, but the compiler kept thinking it was a GPU?
Ava：如果你的加速器能运行 PyTorch 模型，编译器却一直把它当成 GPU，会怎样？

2
00:00:06,918 --> 00:00:08,870
That sounds expensive.
听起来代价不小。

3
00:00:08,870 --> 00:00:09,723
Brian: It can be.
Brian：可以。

4
00:00:09,723 --> 00:00:17,487
IBM's Spyre team moved its torch.compile backend onto Inductor, PyTorch's compiler backend.
IBM 的 Spyre 团队将其 torch.compile 后端迁移到了 PyTorch 的编译器后端 Inductor 上。

5
00:00:17,487 --> 00:00:23,212
The result is roughly seven times smaller than its predecessor.
新后端的规模约为旧版的七分之一。

6
00:00:23,212 --> 00:00:26,922
Ava: Seven times smaller? What did it replace?
Ava：缩小到七分之一？它取代了什么？

7
00:00:26,922 --> 00:00:29,719
Brian: A separate compiler stack called sendnn.
Brian：一套名为 sendnn 的独立编译器。

8
00:00:29,719 --> 00:00:36,992
The new backend extends Inductor, so it can use much of the compiler machinery PyTorch already has.
新后端扩展了 Inductor，因此能复用 PyTorch 现有的大部分编译机制。

9
00:00:36,992 --> 00:00:41,518
Ava: Let's back up. What's unusual about Spyre itself?
Ava：先说说 Spyre 本身有什么特别之处？

10
00:00:41,518 --> 00:00:46,127
Brian: It's a seventy-five-watt accelerator for enterprise inference.
Brian：它是一款功耗 75 瓦的企业级推理加速器。

11
00:00:46,127 --> 00:00:54,060
It connects through PCIe, the computer's expansion bus, and needs no custom power delivery or cooling.
它通过计算机的扩展总线 PCIe 连接，不需要专门的供电或散热设备。

12
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:58,706
Ava: And inside? I'm guessing it doesn't look much like a GPU.
Ava：那内部呢？我猜它和 GPU 很不一样。

13
00:00:58,706 --> 00:01:03,247
Brian: Right. It has thirty-two small cores connected in a ring.
Brian：没错。它有 32 个小型核心，以环形互连。

14
00:01:03,247 --> 00:01:10,528
Each core has its own two-megabyte scratchpad, a small, fast memory managed explicitly.
每个核心都有自己的 2 MB 暂存内存，速度快，但需要显式管理。

15
00:01:10,528 --> 00:01:14,598
Ava: So where does the compiler feel that difference?
Ava：编译器在哪些地方会遇到这种差异？

16
00:01:14,598 --> 00:01:21,559
Brian: In three places: how data moves, what stays in each scratchpad, and which core does which work.
Brian：有三处：数据如何移动、每块暂存内存保留什么，以及每个核心负责什么工作。

17
00:01:21,559 --> 00:01:24,620
Those choices drive this whole story.
这些选择贯穿整个故事。

18
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:30,634
Ava: Before those details, where does torch-spyre fit into the compilation pipeline?
Ava：讲细节前，torch-spyre 在编译流程中处于哪一环？

19
00:01:30,634 --> 00:01:36,264
Brian: Torch-spyre is Spyre's PyTorch integration. Dynamo traces the model.
Brian：Torch-spyre 是 Spyre 的 PyTorch 集成组件。Dynamo 负责追踪模型。

20
00:01:36,264 --> 00:01:38,417
AOTAutograd partitions it.
AOTAutograd 负责分割模型。

21
00:01:38,417 --> 00:01:47,544
Then Inductor lowers the resulting FX graph into a loop-level intermediate representation, or LoopLevelIR.
随后 Inductor 将生成的 FX 图降级为循环级中间表示 LoopLevelIR。

22
00:01:47,544 --> 00:01:53,126
Ava: Intermediate representation meaning the compiler's working version of the program?
Ava：中间表示就是编译器处理程序时使用的内部形式？

23
00:01:53,126 --> 00:01:53,771
Brian: Exactly.
Brian：正是。

24
00:01:53,771 --> 00:02:00,946
Torch-spyre extends that work and produces SuperDSC, another intermediate representation.
Torch-spyre 在此基础上生成另一种中间表示 SuperDSC。

25
00:02:00,946 --> 00:02:08,068
A proprietary compiler called DeepTools then turns SuperDSC into Spyre instructions.
然后，专有编译器 DeepTools 将 SuperDSC 转换为 Spyre 指令。

26
00:02:08,068 --> 00:02:12,546
Ava: So the article focuses on the Inductor-to-SuperDSC part.
Ava：所以文章重点讲 Inductor 到 SuperDSC 的转换。

27
00:02:12,546 --> 00:02:17,926
Brian: Yes. Some extensions break operations into Spyre-friendly primitives.
Brian：对。有些扩展会把操作拆成适合 Spyre 的基础操作。

28
00:02:17,926 --> 00:02:21,980
Others teach Inductor how to lower those primitives.
另一些则让 Inductor 学会如何降级这些基础操作。

29
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:26,696
The big planning decisions happen later, at LoopLevelIR.
关键的规划决策发生在之后的 LoopLevelIR 阶段。

30
00:02:26,696 --> 00:02:31,486
Ava: Is that later planning already a normal Inductor extension hook?
Ava：这个后期规划已经有 Inductor 的常规扩展接口了吗？

31
00:02:31,486 --> 00:02:32,077
Brian: Not yet.
Brian：还没有。

32
00:02:32,077 --> 00:02:42,324
Torch-spyre uses a monkey-patch, meaning it changes an existing code path to insert its passes before Inductor creates SchedulerNodes.
Torch-spyre 使用猴子补丁，修改现有代码路径，在 Inductor 创建 SchedulerNodes 前插入自己的处理流程。

33
00:02:42,324 --> 00:02:44,834
Ava: Why does that timing matter?
Ava：为什么必须赶在那之前？

34
00:02:44,834 --> 00:02:48,858
Brian: Those passes need to rewrite large parts of the graph.
Brian：因为这些处理步骤需要大幅改写计算图。

35
00:02:48,858 --> 00:02:54,298
Once SchedulerNodes exist, Inductor doesn't support that kind of rewrite.
一旦创建了 SchedulerNodes，Inductor 就不支持这种改写了。

36
00:02:54,298 --> 00:02:57,256
The built-in hook arrives too late.
内置扩展接口介入得太晚。

37
00:02:57,256 --> 00:03:02,214
Ava: Got it. Let's take the first hardware problem: moving data.
Ava：明白了。先说第一个硬件问题：数据搬运。

38
00:03:02,214 --> 00:03:04,571
Brian: Spyre moves data in sticks.
Brian：Spyre 以 stick 为单位搬运数据。

39
00:03:04,571 --> 00:03:12,601
Each stick is an aligned chunk of one hundred twenty-eight bytes, or sixty-four fp16 values.
每个 stick 是对齐的 128 字节数据块，包含 64 个 fp16 值。

40
00:03:12,601 --> 00:03:16,268
Fp16 means sixteen-bit floating point.
fp16 指 16 位浮点数。

41
00:03:16,268 --> 00:03:21,058
Ava: Wait, so a kernel can't just ask for one tensor element?
Ava：等等，也就是说内核不能只读取张量中的一个元素？

42
00:03:21,058 --> 00:03:26,086
Brian: That's right. A GPU exposes individual elements to the kernel.
Brian：没错。GPU 允许内核访问单个元素。

43
00:03:26,086 --> 00:03:28,519
Spyre exposes the whole stick.
Spyre 则让内核访问整个 stick。

44
00:03:28,519 --> 00:03:33,816
Think of moving a sealed tray instead of picking up one item.
就像搬动一整盘封好的东西，而不是只拿其中一件。

45
00:03:33,816 --> 00:03:38,582
Ava: Then the compiler has to care how elements are packed into each tray.
Ava：那编译器就得考虑元素在每盘里怎么摆放。

46
00:03:38,582 --> 00:03:39,164
Brian: Exactly.
Brian：正是。

47
00:03:39,164 --> 00:03:46,001
A pointwise operation needs its inputs and output to share the same innermost stick dimension.
逐元素操作要求输入和输出使用相同的最内层 stick 维度。

48
00:03:46,001 --> 00:03:50,860
Different operations can impose different packing constraints.
不同操作可能对数据排布有不同要求。

49
00:03:50,860 --> 00:03:55,506
Ava: What happens when two operations want incompatible packing?
Ava：如果两个操作要求的排布互不兼容呢？

50
00:03:55,506 --> 00:03:58,942
Brian: The compiler may insert a restickify operation.
Brian：编译器可能会插入一个 restickify 重排操作。

51
00:03:58,942 --> 00:04:03,547
It physically rebuilds the sticks with a different arrangement.
它会按另一种排列方式实际重组这些 stick。

52
00:04:03,547 --> 00:04:08,648
That moves data, so choosing layouts across the graph has a cost.
这会搬移数据，因此为整个计算图选择布局是有代价的。

53
00:04:08,648 --> 00:04:13,870
Ava: So the key point is that layout isn't just a local indexing detail.
Ava：所以关键是，布局不只是局部的索引细节。

54
00:04:13,870 --> 00:04:15,761
Brian: Right. It affects real time.
Brian：对，它会影响实际运行时间。

55
00:04:15,761 --> 00:04:26,916
For batched matrix multiplication with tensors already on the device, changing the input and output tiling cut kernel time by twenty-nine to sixty-nine percent.
对于张量已在设备上的批量矩阵乘法，调整输入和输出的分块方式后，内核耗时降低了 29% 到 69%。

56
00:04:26,916 --> 00:04:31,682
Ava: That's the first problem. What's special about the scratchpad?
Ava：这是第一个问题。暂存内存有什么特别之处？

57
00:04:31,682 --> 00:04:34,193
Brian: Nothing moves into it automatically.
Brian：数据不会自动进入暂存内存。

58
00:04:34,193 --> 00:04:43,888
The compiler must emit instructions for transfers from device memory to scratchpad, and from scratchpad to the core's compute engines.
编译器必须生成指令，把数据从设备内存传到暂存内存，再从暂存内存传到核心的计算单元。

59
00:04:43,888 --> 00:04:47,310
Ava: And if it forgets to keep something close?
Ava：如果它没把某个值留在核心附近呢？

60
00:04:47,310 --> 00:04:51,832
Brian: Then that value may go back to device memory and get fetched again.
Brian：那个值就可能被写回设备内存，之后再取回来。

61
00:04:51,832 --> 00:04:57,716
Spyre workloads are often limited by memory movement, so those round trips matter.
Spyre 的工作负载常受数据搬移限制，所以这些往返传输很重要。

62
00:04:57,716 --> 00:05:01,690
Ava: The third problem was dividing work among cores, right?
Ava：第三个问题是如何在各核心间分配工作，对吧？

63
00:05:01,690 --> 00:05:04,839
Brian: Yes. Spyre is a static dataflow machine.
Brian：对。Spyre 是静态数据流机器。

64
00:05:04,839 --> 00:05:10,507
It doesn't have a GPU-style runtime scheduler balancing work as it goes.
它没有像 GPU 那样边运行边平衡工作的运行时调度器。

65
00:05:10,507 --> 00:05:15,816
The compiler assigns each operation's work to cores beforehand.
编译器会预先把每个操作的工作分配给各核心。

66
00:05:15,816 --> 00:05:19,214
Ava: Does that fixed assignment offer any upside?
Ava：这种固定分配有什么好处吗？

67
00:05:19,214 --> 00:05:19,713
Brian: It can.
Brian：有可能。

68
00:05:19,713 --> 00:05:28,828
These are naturally persistent kernels: a core can keep useful data in its scratchpad across several rounds of computation.
这些天然就是持久化内核：核心可以在多轮计算中把有用的数据留在暂存内存里。

69
00:05:28,828 --> 00:05:31,722
Ava: How does the compiler decide the split?
Ava：编译器怎么决定如何拆分？

70
00:05:31,722 --> 00:05:35,307
Brian: Its work-division planner makes three passes.
Brian：工作划分规划器会执行三轮处理。

71
00:05:35,307 --> 00:05:39,929
First it enforces each core's device-memory address limit.
第一轮确保不超出每个核心的设备内存寻址限制。

72
00:05:39,929 --> 00:05:44,390
Then a cost model compares matrix multiplication splits.
然后，成本模型比较不同的矩阵乘法拆分方案。

73
00:05:44,390 --> 00:05:47,768
A final pass handles other operations.
最后一轮处理其他操作。

74
00:05:47,768 --> 00:05:52,870
Ava: Can you make that concrete without reading a page of tensor shapes?
Ava：不用念一整页张量形状，能举个具体例子吗？

75
00:05:52,870 --> 00:05:59,313
Brian: In the article's large matmul example, the first pass must split the reduction dimension in two.
Brian：在文章的大型矩阵乘法示例中，第一轮必须把归约维度拆成两份。

76
00:05:59,313 --> 00:06:04,428
The cost model then favors splitting the output dimension sixteen ways.
成本模型随后倾向于把输出维度拆成 16 份。

77
00:06:04,428 --> 00:06:08,762
Ava: So it picks a legal split, then looks for a faster one.
Ava：所以先选可行的拆分方式，再寻找更快的方案。

78
00:06:08,762 --> 00:06:12,720
Brian: Exactly. Splitting can also make data fit locally.
Brian：没错。拆分还能让数据放进核心的暂存内存。

79
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:21,304
A four-megabyte tensor won't fit in one core's scratchpad, but four row slices of one megabyte each can.
一个 4 MB 的张量放不进单个核心的暂存内存，但切成四个各 1 MB 的行切片就可以。

80
00:06:21,304 --> 00:06:25,038
Ava: What if even a core's slice is too large?
Ava：如果单个核心的切片还是太大呢？

81
00:06:25,038 --> 00:06:28,241
Brian: The program can iterate over smaller tiles.
Brian：程序可以按更小的分块迭代。

82
00:06:28,241 --> 00:06:34,275
Torch-spyre offers a helper built on torch.scan for expressing those tiled loops.
Torch-spyre 提供了基于 torch.scan 的辅助工具，用于编写这类分块循环。

83
00:06:34,275 --> 00:06:39,020
That trades more iterations for a smaller memory footprint.
这样会增加迭代次数，但减少内存占用。

84
00:06:39,020 --> 00:06:43,162
Ava: And then an allocator chooses what stays in scratchpad?
Ava：接着由分配器决定哪些数据留在暂存内存里？

85
00:06:43,162 --> 00:06:45,946
Brian: Yes, including where each buffer sits.
Brian：对，还会决定每个缓冲区放在哪里。

86
00:06:45,946 --> 00:06:56,112
It offers solvers from a simple greedy method to more involved options, including simulated annealing and CP-SAT, a constraint solver.
它提供多种求解方法，从简单的贪心算法到模拟退火和约束求解器 CP-SAT 等复杂方案。

87
00:06:56,112 --> 00:07:01,238
Ava: Let's put numbers on it. Did scratchpad planning help an actual kernel?
Ava：说点具体数字吧。暂存内存规划真的改善了内核性能吗？

88
00:07:01,238 --> 00:07:11,360
Brian: In a single-core softmax example, improved scheduling cut median kernel time from thirty-two point five one to twenty-three point six seven microseconds.
Brian：在单核心 softmax 示例中，改进调度后，内核耗时中位数从 32.51 微秒降至 23.67 微秒。

89
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:14,356
That's a twenty-seven percent reduction.
这相当于降低了 27%。

90
00:07:14,356 --> 00:07:17,322
Ava: What changed in that softmax?
Ava：这个 softmax 做了哪些改变？

91
00:07:17,322 --> 00:07:29,312
Brian: The compiler kept small reduction outputs on-core, reused scratchpad addresses for pointwise steps, and copied the input into scratchpad once instead of fetching it for every reader.
Brian：编译器把较小的归约结果留在核心上，让逐元素计算复用暂存内存地址，并且只把输入复制到暂存内存一次，供所有读取操作使用。

92
00:07:29,312 --> 00:07:35,062
Ava: That sounds clear enough. Where do work division and storage start fighting?
Ava：明白了。工作划分和数据存放从哪里开始冲突？

93
00:07:35,062 --> 00:07:39,534
Brian: Imagine one operation produces a buffer across several cores.
Brian：假设一个操作在多个核心上生成了一个缓冲区。

94
00:07:39,534 --> 00:07:42,832
The next operation chooses a different split.
下一个操作却选择了另一种拆分方式。

95
00:07:42,832 --> 00:07:48,900
Its cores expect different pieces from the ones already sitting in scratchpad.
它的各个核心需要的数据块，与已经存放在暂存内存里的不一样。

96
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:54,458
Ava: Ah, a work-division mismatch. What did the compiler do before?
Ava：啊，工作划分不匹配。以前编译器怎么处理？

97
00:07:54,458 --> 00:07:57,625
Brian: It couldn't keep that shared buffer resident.
Brian：它无法让这个共享缓冲区常驻核心。

98
00:07:57,625 --> 00:08:02,551
The producer wrote it to device memory, and the consumer read it back.
生产者把它写入设备内存，消费者再读回来。

99
00:08:02,551 --> 00:08:07,264
That's exactly the traffic scratchpad planning tries to avoid.
这正是暂存内存规划想避免的数据传输。

100
00:08:07,264 --> 00:08:11,310
Ava: Is there a way to move pieces directly between cores?
Ava：能不能直接在核心之间搬移数据块？

101
00:08:11,310 --> 00:08:12,417
Brian: Spyre has one.
Brian：Spyre 有一个。

102
00:08:12,417 --> 00:08:19,295
Torch-spyre has begun using lx_relayout, an on-chip shuffle between cores' scratchpads.
Torch-spyre 已开始使用 lx_relayout，在各核心的片上暂存区之间重排数据。

103
00:08:19,295 --> 00:08:25,100
It still takes time and needs space for both layouts during the move.
这仍要花时间，搬移时还得为两种布局留出空间。

104
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:28,978
Ava: So the compiler now has a real choice to price.
Ava：所以编译器现在有了需要权衡成本的选择。

105
00:08:28,978 --> 00:08:29,407
Brian: Right.
Brian：对。

106
00:08:29,407 --> 00:08:39,048
It can accept a less efficient split that keeps neighboring operations aligned, pay for an on-chip relayout, or use device memory.
它可以接受效率较低、但能让相邻算子布局一致的划分，也可以付出片上重排的代价，或使用设备内存。

107
00:08:39,048 --> 00:08:42,494
Ava: How does it compare choices across a whole chain?
Ava：它怎么比较整条计算链上的选择？

108
00:08:42,494 --> 00:08:49,302
Brian: A newer co-optimizing allocator chooses work division and scratchpad placement together.
Brian：一种较新的协同优化分配器会同时决定任务划分和暂存区布局。

109
00:08:49,302 --> 00:08:53,634
One path prunes the search with hardware-specific rules.
一种方法用硬件专用规则缩小搜索范围。

110
00:08:53,634 --> 00:08:59,476
Another uses OR-Tools CP-SAT to examine candidate splits and relayouts.
另一种方法用 OR-Tools CP-SAT 检查候选划分和重排方案。

111
00:08:59,476 --> 00:09:04,362
Ava: Does the fuller search account for scratchpad capacity during a shuffle?
Ava：更全面的搜索会考虑重排时的暂存区容量吗？

112
00:09:04,362 --> 00:09:04,676
Brian: Yes.
Brian：对。

113
00:09:04,676 --> 00:09:11,496
Its constraints require buffers to fit, including both copies while a relayout is live.
它的约束要求缓冲区都装得下，包括重排期间同时存在的两份数据。

114
00:09:11,496 --> 00:09:17,648
It also assigns relayout a cost, rather than treating the shuffle as free.
它还会给重排计入成本，不会把数据搬移当成免费的。

115
00:09:17,648 --> 00:09:20,854
Ava: What difference has coordinated planning made?
Ava：协同规划带来了什么变化？

116
00:09:20,854 --> 00:09:29,194
Brian: In a fused multilayer perceptron, or MLP, style kernel, a pointwise operation adopted a neighboring matmul's split.
Brian：在一个融合的多层感知机（MLP）式内核中，逐点算子采用了相邻矩阵乘法的划分方式。

117
00:09:29,194 --> 00:09:34,620
Core utilization rose from roughly two-thirds to about eighty percent.
核心利用率从约三分之二升至约百分之八十。

118
00:09:34,620 --> 00:09:36,530
Ava: And kernel time?
Ava：内核耗时呢？

119
00:09:36,530 --> 00:09:39,206
Brian: It fell by around seventeen percent.
Brian：缩短了约百分之十七。

120
00:09:39,206 --> 00:09:45,280
That kernel was also roughly twice as fast as its sendnn-compiled equivalent.
这个内核也比对应的 sendnn 编译版本快了约一倍。

121
00:09:45,280 --> 00:09:48,990
Ava: Does matching splits work for every chain?
Ava：让划分方式一致对每条计算链都有效吗？

122
00:09:48,990 --> 00:09:56,113
Brian: No. In a multi-head-attention kernel, the matmuls around softmax contract along different axes.
Brian：不对。在多头注意力内核中，softmax 前后的矩阵乘法沿不同的轴进行缩并。

123
00:09:56,113 --> 00:09:59,612
They can't simply copy each other's split.
它们不能直接照搬彼此的划分方式。

124
00:09:59,612 --> 00:10:02,218
Ava: What did the planner find instead?
Ava：规划器找到了什么办法？

125
00:10:02,218 --> 00:10:07,257
Brian: It offered both matmuls and the softmax a shared batch-and-output tiling.
Brian：它为两个矩阵乘法和 softmax 选用了共同的批次维与输出维分块方案。

126
00:10:07,257 --> 00:10:14,856
The chain then used one consistent division, keeping the attention scores off a device-memory round trip.
整条计算链因此采用一致的划分方式，避免注意力分数往返设备内存。

127
00:10:14,856 --> 00:10:18,110
Ava: And where did direct relayout pay off?
Ava：直接重排在哪里带来了收益？

128
00:10:18,110 --> 00:10:26,896
Brian: On a small synthetic graph, an eighty-microsecond device-memory round trip became a one point one microsecond on-chip shuffle.
Brian：在一个小型合成计算图上，原本耗时八十微秒的设备内存往返，变成了一点一微秒的片上重排。

129
00:10:26,896 --> 00:10:31,012
That's a focused example, not a general speedup claim.
这只是一个特定案例，不能据此推断普遍加速效果。

130
00:10:31,012 --> 00:10:35,298
Ava: Let's return to that seven-times-smaller compiler claim.
Ava：再说说编译器体量缩小约七倍的说法。

131
00:10:35,298 --> 00:10:46,016
Brian: The article counts roughly two hundred seventy-three thousand non-comment lines for sendnn and thirty-eight thousand six hundred for the Inductor extension, excluding tests.
Brian：文章统计，sendnn 约有二十七万三千行非注释代码，Inductor 扩展约有三万八千六百行，均不含测试代码。

132
00:10:46,016 --> 00:10:50,950
Ava: Is that a direct comparison of equal, standalone compilers?
Ava：这是在直接比较两个功能对等、可独立运行的编译器吗？

133
00:10:50,950 --> 00:10:57,149
Brian: No. The extension relies on Inductor underneath it. That's why it can be smaller.
Brian：不是。这个扩展依赖底层的 Inductor，所以代码量才更小。

134
00:10:57,149 --> 00:11:03,852
The article also reports fewer functions and lower average complexity per function.
文章还指出，它的函数更少，每个函数的平均复杂度也更低。

135
00:11:03,852 --> 00:11:05,978
Ava: What still needs work?
Ava：还有哪些工作要做？

136
00:11:05,978 --> 00:11:11,365
Brian: The exhaustive search grows quickly as kernels grow, so its run time is bounded.
Brian：内核越大，穷举搜索的规模增长越快，因此必须限制运行时间。

137
00:11:11,365 --> 00:11:17,776
The team is exploring pruning rules that keep searches fast without losing good solutions.
团队正探索剪枝规则，在保留优质方案的同时加快搜索。

138
00:11:17,776 --> 00:11:20,958
Ava: And the Inductor hook still needs cleaning up?
Ava：Inductor 的接入方式也还需要改进？

139
00:11:20,958 --> 00:11:21,235
Brian: Yes.
Brian：对。

140
00:11:21,235 --> 00:11:30,716
Turning those pre-scheduling passes from a monkey-patch into a supported hook is under discussion with upstream PyTorch maintainers.
团队正与上游 PyTorch 维护者讨论，把这些调度前处理从猴子补丁改成正式支持的扩展钩子。

141
00:11:30,716 --> 00:11:34,234
Ava: Has any of this work already reached upstream?
Ava：这些工作已有部分进入上游了吗？

142
00:11:34,234 --> 00:11:41,403
Brian: Support for registering device-specific decompositions improved and shipped in PyTorch two point twelve.
Brian：设备专用分解规则的注册支持已有改进，并随 PyTorch 2.12 发布。

143
00:11:41,403 --> 00:11:49,128
The article also describes public design proposals for tiled layouts and a new intermediate representation.
文章还介绍了关于分块布局和新中间表示的公开设计提案。

144
00:11:49,128 --> 00:11:51,806
Ava: Give me the three-point recap.
Ava：用三点总结一下。

145
00:11:51,806 --> 00:11:56,566
Brian: First, Inductor gave torch-spyre a much smaller compiler base.
Brian：第一，Inductor 为 torch-spyre 提供了规模小得多的编译器基础。

146
00:11:56,566 --> 00:12:02,708
Second, sticks, scratchpads, and static core assignment demand special planning.
第二，stick 数据块、暂存区和静态核心分配都需要专门规划。

147
00:12:02,708 --> 00:12:07,972
Third, coordinating those decisions avoids costly data movement.
第三，协调这些决策可以避免代价高昂的数据搬移。

148
00:12:07,972 --> 00:12:12,690
Ava: That's Spyre and Inductor for today. Thanks for listening.
Ava：今天关于 Spyre 和 Inductor 的内容就到这里。感谢收听。
